The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
kfree(channel);
list_add_tail(&channel->percpu_list, &hv_cpu->chan_list);
list_del(&channel->percpu_list);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `kfree(channel);` statement can be executed while the per-cpu channel list is being accessed in an interrupt routine. If an interrupt occurs during the deletion of a channel, it may lead to accessing a freed channel, resulting in a use-after-free condition.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
kfree_rcu(channel, rcu);
list_add_tail_rcu(&channel->percpu_list, &hv_cpu->chan_list);
list_del_rcu(&channel->percpu_list);
```
Reason:
The fix uses RCU (Read-Copy-Update) mechanisms to safely manage the per-cpu channel list. The `kfree_rcu(channel, rcu);` ensures that the channel is only freed after all references to it are done, preventing use-after-free issues. The `list_add_tail_rcu` and `list_del_rcu` functions ensure that additions and deletions to the list are done in a way that allows concurrent access without risking invalid memory access.

